SONG 1 — REGGAE: a song about the landlord knocking while i'm counting change on the table
======================================================================

----- A (13.2s) -----
[verse]
You knock on the door like you own the place.
You shout my name with a frown on your face.
I'm sitting here by the kitchen light.
I'm trying to make these pennies right.

[chorus]
Wait a little bit.
Just a little bit more.
Leave me alone by the door.

[verse]
You say the rent is two weeks late.
You say you're tired of the way I wait.
But look at the table, see the silver shine.
I'm counting every cent, one at a time.

[chorus]
Wait a little bit.
Just a little bit more.
Leave me alone by the door.

[verse]
Now you're talking 'bout the court in town.
You want to tear my little shelter down.
But I got a check coming from the harbor pier.
It'll be in my hand by the end of the year.

[chorus]
Wait a little bit.
Just a little bit more.
Leave me alone by the door.

[verse]
Stop your shouting, go back to your car.
You know where the bank and the liquor store are.
I got forty dollars and a handful of dime.
You'll get your money in a little more time.

[chorus]
Wait a little bit.
Just a little bit more.
Leave me alone by the door.

----- B (6.5s) -----
[verse]
The knock comes hard on the door frame three times
I freeze up right there by the table
I got four quarters and two dimes and a crumpled five
Trying to make the number look bigger than it is
The deadbolt slides with a heavy metallic sound
And there he is standing in the hallway light
He doesn’t say hello, he just looks at my shoes
He knows I’m broke before I even speak

[chorus]
Rent is due on the first but it’s twenty-three now
So you can keep your key and take your time
I ain’t got no money and I ain’t got no pride
Just this floor and these clothes and this story

[verse]
He leans against the doorjamb and checks his watch
Asks if I’m planning to stay for dinner
I tell him I’m waiting on a check from the yard
He laughs like I’m telling a joke from last week
He says the water shut off at the building next door
And asks if I got any tanks left for the shower
I hand him the five dollars and the two dimes
He takes them without looking at my eyes

[chorus]
Rent is due on the first but it’s twenty-three now
So you can keep your key and take your time
I ain’t got no money and I ain’t got no pride
Just this floor and these clothes and this story

[verse]
He walks away down the concrete stairs
His boots make a rhythmic thud on the steps
I pick up the change and count it again
Just to see if it grows if I stare long enough
The refrigerator hums like a tired dog
And the silence gets loud in the kitchen
I put the coins back in the jar by the sink
And wait for the rain to wash the dust off the window

