SONG 2 — HIP-HOP: a song about my grandma's house getting sold
======================================================================

----- A (2.0s) -----
[verse]
The "Sold" sign nailed in the oak tree blocks your view.
I walked up the driveway and I didn't say a word.
The realtor’s got a smile that doesn’t reach his eyes.
He kept checking his watch like he had a better place to be.

[chorus]
They bought the roof, they bought the walls.
They didn’t buy the ghost who lives in the hall.

[verse]
You packed your china in the boxes labeled "Fragile."
I found the chip in the saucer you dropped back in '98.
The kitchen smells like lemon cleaner now, not your frying bacon.
The new owners want to tear out the hardwood floors for carpet.

[chorus]
They bought the roof, they bought the walls.
They didn’t buy the ghost who lives in the hall.

----- B (16.7s) -----
[verse]
You told me the papers are already signed.
You didn't even ask me before you called the man.
Now there's a plastic sign sticking in the dirt
Right where the hydrangea used to grow.
You say the taxes are too high for us to pay,
But you forget who scrubbed those kitchen floors.
You forget who taught me how to peel a potato
While the radio played some old gospel song.

[chorus]
It ain't a house no more.
It's just a number on a page.
It ain't a house no more.

[verse]
I went back there on Tuesday just to look.
Some stranger in a suit was walking through the hall.
He touched the wallpaper in the guest room
Like he was deciding where to put a painting.
He didn't know about the leak in the upstairs bath,
Or the way the third step creaks when you hit it wrong.
He looked at me and asked if I was the help,
And I almost punched him in his polished teeth.

[chorus]
It ain't a house no more.
It's just a number on a page.
It ain't a house no more.

[verse]
Now you're putting her things in cardboard boxes.
You put her favorite quilt in a black trash bag.
You say we need to move fast before the closing date,
But I'm still smelling cinnamon in the curtains.
You can sell the land and you can sell the roof,
You can sell the porch where we sat every July.
But you can't sell the way she looked at me
When she told me I was the only one who got it right.

[chorus]
It ain't a house no more.
It's just a number on a page.
It ain't a house no more.

